avg rtt count (~4.49): The average number of RDMA round-trips needed per operation. Each key lookup traverses the remote ART index, and on average it takes ~4.5 one-sided RDMA reads/CAS operations to complete a single search. This includes hashing into the RACE directory, following the pointer to a segment, then traversing ART nodes down to the leaf. Lower is better — it means fewer network hops per query.

avg bandwidth consumption (~1820): The average number of bytes transferred over RDMA per operation. With ~4.5 round-trips reading nodes of sizes 64–1095 bytes each, about 1.8 KB of network data is moved per lookup. This measures the network cost of each operation — important because RDMA NIC bandwidth is a shared, finite resource. Under high thread counts, total bandwidth = 1820 bytes × 1.678M ops/s ≈ 3.05 GB/s aggregate across all 30 threads.


DART has no data cache, so every operation incurs the full ~4.5 RTTs and ~1.8 KB transfer. This is constant regardless of skew (zipf 0.5 vs 0.99 gives identical numbers).
DEX with its B-tree cache can serve hot keys from local memory with 0 RTTs, which is why it achieves higher throughput under skewed workloads despite having a larger index structure.
